assertion  XSH.rint.001
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       While the rounding direction is toward negative infinity, toward
           positive infinity or toward zero (each one that can be selected; at
           least one is needed), rint() of -3.5, -2.75, -2.5, -1.5, -1.25, -0.75,
           0.75, 1.25, 1.5, 2.5, 2.75 and 3.5 gives what floor(), ceil() or
           trunc() gives respectively.
test       t/001-directed.c

assertion  XSH.rint.002
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       While the rounding direction is to nearest, halfway cases go to the
           even neighbour: rint(0.5) is 0, rint(1.5) is 2, rint(2.5) is 2,
           rint(3.5) is 4, rint(4.5) is 4 and the negative counterparts; other
           values go to the nearest integer.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The test is NOTINUSE when to-nearest cannot be selected.
test       t/002-nearest-even.c

assertion  XSH.rint.003
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       In every selectable rounding direction, rint(-0.75), rint(-0.25),
           rint(0.25) and rint(0.75) have the same sign as the argument, even
           when the result is zero.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-sign.c

assertion  XSH.rint.004
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       A NaN argument gives a NaN result.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-nan.c

assertion  XSH.rint.005
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For +0 and -0 the result is the argument, with its sign.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-zero.c

assertion  XSH.rint.006
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       For +Inf and -Inf the result is the argument.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/006-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.rint.007
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       In every selectable rounding direction (at least one is needed),
           rint(x) and nearbyint(x) have the same value for x in -3.5, -2.75,
           -2.5, -1.5, -0.75, 0.75, 1.5, 2.5, 2.75 and 3.5.
test       t/007-equals-nearbyint.c

assertion  XSH.rint.008
anchor     functions/rint.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/rint.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, rint(2.5) leaves it unchanged
           and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the invalid,
           divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/008-no-error.c
